WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:06.200
Wow, I can’t believe it! This new&nbsp;
AI can look at a real tennis player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:12.720
and put them in a computer game where we can&nbsp;
play with them. This looks absolutely amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.920
and doing this the hard way has&nbsp;
so far been absolutely impossible.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:26.400
But what about the easy way? Exciting as this may&nbsp;
seem, perhaps there is nothing new here. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.080
we can already do something like this&nbsp;
through motion capture. What is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.200
Well, you put a human in a studio,&nbsp;
attach these little balls to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.480
and record their motion. Then, transfer&nbsp;
this motion to a virtual character. Easy.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.840
However, this paper has an incredible twist.&nbsp;
And that twist is that this can do the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:57.680
but without the balls. No cameras, gyroscopes,&nbsp;
or any additional machinery is required. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:05.480
the raw video feed, and the learning happens&nbsp;
from that. That sounds incredible - but how?

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.920
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.360
Well, there are previous works&nbsp;
that try to do something similar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:20.760
but the precision of these motions was almost&nbsp;
never satisfactory. Especially for tennis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.720
where almost hitting a ball is not&nbsp;
nearly good enough. I am very proud to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:33.160
say that all three of these papers have been&nbsp;
showcased here on Two Minute Papers before.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.680
But this new technique, this one&nbsp;
can do it even better. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:45.160
we give it the raw video feed of the players, and&nbsp;
then, it will try to estimate their motion. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:52.920
are we done? Well, unfortunately not. Not even&nbsp;
close. Look! This motion is still way too jerky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:02:00.200
and imprecise to be used in practice.&nbsp;
It is full of artifacts. So, what now?

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.440
Well, now comes the magic! Here, a&nbsp;
computer simulation is built that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.280
tries to reproduce this motion, but&nbsp;
this one cannot be jerky because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.120
is within a video game where the laws&nbsp;
of physics are intact. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:24.440
people don’t move like that. And with this&nbsp;
simulation, hmm… this is looking so much better!

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.680
From now on, we can take a target&nbsp;
position that we start from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.440
and simulate many-many possible motions types.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:38.120
Then, we can grab a controller and perform&nbsp;
these serves, top spin, back spin hits, slices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:43.800
you name it. And they look absolutely&nbsp;
incredible. Yes, I hear you asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:49.640
Károly, are you sure that this is a&nbsp;
simulation? Well, if you look here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:56.000
here is the proof. Yes, simulations today&nbsp;
are getting so advanced that we need to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:03.280
these 3D models to be absolutely sure that&nbsp;
this is indeed a computer simulation. Wow.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.280
And it gets better. Here is the interesting&nbsp;
part: this is not just a copying machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.320
although just copying the motion from&nbsp;
these videos would also be quite a feat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:20.560
given the occlusions and tiny, but super&nbsp;
important wrist motions that are really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.640
hard to see from the footage. But with this&nbsp;
system, we can even highlight a region where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.360
we would like to hit the ball, and the&nbsp;
motions will be synthesized accordingly.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.440
We can run the same simulation with a&nbsp;
number of different target locations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:43.160
and all of the synthesized motions will be&nbsp;
believable, and accurate. Loving it. Or we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:49.960
can also ask the player to hit the ball to the&nbsp;
same point from different starting positions.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:53.560
And it will work not only for the easier cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:59.760
but in more difficult scenarios too where&nbsp;
you can barely catch that ball. So good!

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.560
And now, hold on to your papers Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars, because it can not only do all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:11.120
but it can even learn the individual&nbsp;
style of famous tennis players.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.187
Right and left-handed players work too,&nbsp;
and it can even simulate a one or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.187 --> 00:04:21.280
handed backhand shot, depending on&nbsp;
the style of the player at hand.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:28.200
And if that is possible at all, it gets even&nbsp;
better! If we can simulate one virtual human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:34.160
why not make a game by simulating two&nbsp;
playing against each other? I have to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.560
if I look closely, I can still see that this&nbsp;
is a simulation, but if you squint a little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.480
it’s easy to think that this is&nbsp;
real footage. That is an incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:51.760
achievement both in the graphics, and&nbsp;
in the animation departments. Bravo!

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.640
Styles are particularly interesting here,&nbsp;
because if it would have only one style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:02.440
two players playing that same style&nbsp;
would not look nearly as interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.560
at this footage with one left&nbsp;
and one right-handed player.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.440
What I particularly like is that&nbsp;
there is barely any foot sliding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:17.920
a problem that you see in almost every single&nbsp;
paper in this area. They usually compete on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:25.240
how little foot sliding there is, no foot&nbsp;
sliding at all is almost unheard of. Wow. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:30.680
almost none of the jitters from the original&nbsp;
motions are there in this footage. The foot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:37.680
sliding here is less than an inch, and the&nbsp;
jitter is almost nonexistent. All gone.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.520
And if we remove just one small&nbsp;
part of this new proposed system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.480
things can go really bad. For instance,&nbsp;
if the wrist motions are captured with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:53.680
just a bit of inaccuracy, the hits will go&nbsp;
completely wrong. You have to get everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.800
nearly perfectly for this to work. And&nbsp;
the scientists absolutely nailed it.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:05.680
So, how many hundreds of hours did the AI&nbsp;
have to sift through to learn all this? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.720
it was not in the order of hundreds of hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:16.840
but get this, only 4.5 hours of video footage.&nbsp;
Goodness. I don’t even know what to say.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:22.440
When using the full system, the hit rates can&nbsp;
reach up to a hundred percent, which sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:28.920
almost impossible to me. They absolutely knocked&nbsp;
this out of the court with this paper, and thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:35.240
it was published at SIGGRAPH, perhaps the most&nbsp;
prestigious conference in computer graphics. In my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:42.160
view, it is like winning the olympic gold medal&nbsp;
of computer graphics research. I have to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:48.080
it is very well deserved. Bravo! This&nbsp;
might be the future of video games.

